# .claude/verify-harness -- the command that proves this repo works.
#
# WHO RUNS IT. The Stop gate (scripts/require-verification.py) runs this file
# when the agent changed code and nothing has verified it since. It takes every
# line that is not a comment, runs them as ONE `sh` script from the repo root,
# and reads the exit code:
#   0          verified -- the turn may end
#   non-zero   NOT verified -- the turn is refused, with the tail of the output
# /verify (scripts/verify.sh) runs it too, when the repo has no app harness.
#
# THE ONE RULE. Exit non-zero whenever anything is unverified, and never exit 0
# having checked nothing. A harness that passes on an empty test suite, a
# skipped step or a missing tool is worse than none: it turns "unverified" into
# "verified" with a green tick.
#   - `set -e`, so a failing step fails the harness.
#   - Run the real test command, not a wrapper that swallows its exit code.
#   - No `|| true`, no `2>/dev/null` on a command whose success you rely on.
#   - A file of only comments is refused by the gate as "empty".
#
# Plain sh, not bash: no arrays, no [[ ]]. Keep it inside the gate's time
# budget (300s by default); a slow harness is refused, never killed mid-run.
# Generate a starting point instead of copying this file by hand:
#   scripts/scaffold-verification-skill.sh <repo-path>
#
# THREE SHAPES. Uncomment one, adapt it, delete the rest -- and delete the
# refusal at the bottom, which is what keeps an unedited copy from passing.
#
# --- 1. tests only (a library) -------------------------------------------------
# The project's own checks ARE the verification. Cheapest first.
#
#   set -e
#   npm run lint
#   npm run typecheck
#   npm test
#
# Other ecosystems, same idea:
#   python3 -m pytest              # or: uv run pytest / poetry run pytest
#   go vet ./... && go test ./...  # make sure *_test.go files exist: with none,
#                                  # `go test` passes having run nothing
#   cargo test
#
# --- 2. a CLI ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The checks, plus a run of the real entrypoint, so a broken build or a crash on
# start-up cannot hide behind green unit tests.
#
#   set -e
#   python3 -m pytest
#   python3 -m mytool --help >/dev/null
#   out=$(python3 -m mytool add 2 3)
#   [ "$out" = "5" ] || { echo "mytool add 2 3 printed '$out', wanted 5" >&2; exit 1; }
#
# --- 3. a web app ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The checks, then the app itself, through the app harness that
# scaffold-verification-skill.sh generates. Its `verify` verb finds the kit
# (BENDIT_KIT_ROOT, CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT when it is this kit, a copy vendored
# in the repo, the newest installed copy -- `kit` says which) and runs
# verify.sh: start the app on this repo's registry port, wait until it SERVES
# (a 5xx is not ready), run doctor, run every scenarios/*.sh, tear it down.
# Zero scenarios is a failure, not a pass. No env var or local file is needed,
# so it runs the same in a terminal, under the Stop gate and in a fresh clone.
#
#   set -e
#   npm run lint
#   npm test
#   "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)/.claude/skills/verify-myapp/bin/verify-myapp" verify
#
# --- delete this line once a shape above is in place -----------------------------
echo "verify-harness: this is the unedited template -- pick a shape above, then delete this line" >&2; exit 2
